.TH DOIT 1 "September 2026" "DoIT" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
doit \- A sleek CLI task manager tool built with Go and WASM SQLite3
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B doit
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B doit
is a command-line todo/task manager that stores its data in a local SQLite
database (see
.B init
and
.B DBPATH
below). Tasks live in one of four logical lists:
.IR todos ,
.IR completed ,
.IR trash ,
and
.IR ignored .
Every list command clears the terminal screen before printing, so scrollback is
not preserved.
.SH INITIALIZATION
Before using
.B doit
in a directory tree, you must initialize a repository:
.PP
.B doit init
.PP
This creates a
.B .doit/
directory containing the SQLite database
.B doit.db
in the current working directory, and refuses to run if
.B .doit/
already exists there. Every later
.B doit
command looks for
.B .doit/
by starting from the current directory and walking upward, so commands will
only work when run inside (or below) an initialized project. The schema is only
applied by
.BR doit init ;
later versions of
.B doit
do not upgrade an existing database.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR init
Create a new
.B .doit
repository in the current directory.
.TP
.BR add " | " a " " \fItask\fP " [" \fI-t=deadline\fP " ]"
Add a new todo. With a single task argument this adds one task. With more than
one argument it switches to multi-task mode; see
.B ADDING TASKS
below.
.TP
.BR list " | " ls
List all active todos. Tasks are numbered starting from index 0; these
indices (not database row ids) are the ids used by other commands.
.TP
.BR done " | " d " " \fIid\fP
Mark the todo at list index
.I id
as done, moving it to the
.IR completed
list.
.TP
.BR remove " | " rm " " \fIid\fP
Prompt for confirmation, then move the todo at list index
.I id
to the
.IR trash
list.
.TP
.BR nuke " | " n
Permanently clear the entire
.IR todos
list, after a two-step confirmation (see
.BR "DESTRUCTIVE COMMANDS" ).
This is a top-level command; the
.IR completed ,
.IR trash ,
and
.I ignored
lists are cleared through their own
.B nuke
subcommands.
.TP
.BR completed " | " c " " \fIsubcommand\fP
Operate on the completed list. Subcommands:
.RS
.TP
.BR list " | " ls
List completed tasks.
.TP
.BR remove " | " rm " " \fIid\fP
Permanently delete the task at index
.I id
from the completed list (cannot be recovered).
.TP
.BR nuke " | " n
Permanently clear all completed tasks, after a two-step confirmation.
.RE
.TP
.BR trash " | " t " " \fIsubcommand\fP
Operate on the trash list. Subcommands:
.RS
.TP
.BR list " | " ls
List tasks in trash.
.TP
.BR remove " | " rm " " \fIid\fP
Permanently delete the task at index
.I id
from the trash (cannot be recovered). See
.B BUGS
for a known defect in this subcommand.
.TP
.BR nuke " | " n
Permanently clear the entire trash, after a two-step confirmation.
.RE
.TP
.BR ignored " | " i " " \fIsubcommand\fP
Operate on the ignored list. Subcommands:
.RS
.TP
.BR list " | " ls
List ignored tasks.
.TP
.BR remove " | " rm " " \fIid\fP
Permanently delete the task at index
.I id
from the ignored list (cannot be recovered).
.TP
.BR nuke " | " n
Permanently clear all ignored tasks, after a two-step confirmation.
.RE
.TP
.BR move " | " mv " " \fI<t|c|i>\fP " " \fIid\fP " [" \fI<t|c|i>\fP "]"
Move a task between the trash, completed, and ignored lists. The first
source argument
.I <t|c|i>
selects the source list (one of
.IR trash ,
.IR completed ,
.I ignored
or their single-letter aliases), and
.I id
is the 0-based index within that list. With no target argument, the task is
moved back to
.IR todos .
With an explicit target
.I <t|c|i>
it is moved to that list instead.
.SH ADDING TASKS
A single task is added by quoting it as one argument:
.PP
.RS
.nf
doit add "buy milk"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Supplying more than one argument switches
.B add
to multi-task mode, where tasks are separated by a
.B comma
that must be its own whitespace-delimited argument, i.e. surrounded by spaces
or quoted on its own. Each task may be followed by an optional deadline
written as
.BR -t= ,
for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
doit add taskA -t=2h , "task B" -t=1mo
.fi
.RE
.PP
This adds both tasks in the order given. The separator must be surrounded by
whitespace (or quoted on its own); a comma attached to a task is treated as
part of that task's text and produces an error.
.PP
Each added task prints its
.I database row id
under an
.B ID:
heading. This is
.I not
the list index that
.BR done ,
.BR remove ,
.BR mv ,
and the
.B remove
subcommands expect; use the index shown by the list commands instead.
.SH DESTRUCTIVE COMMANDS
.IR remove ,
.IR rm ,
and the per-table
.B remove
subcommands prompt once and accept
.BR y ,
.B Y
as confirmation and
.BR n ,
.B N
as cancellation, in any case. Any other key aborts with an error.
.PP
Every
.B nuke
command uses a two-step confirmation. First a
.B (Y/N)?
prompt that accepts only a strict uppercase
.BR Y ;
.B n
or
.B N
cancels, and any other input is rejected. After
.BR Y ,
a typed confirmation phrase is required. The phrases are case-sensitive and
must be entered exactly:
.TP
.B doit nuke
YeS NuKe ToDos
.TP
.B doit completed nuke
YeS NuKe CoMpleteD
.TP
.B doit trash nuke
YeS NuKe TrAsH
.TP
.B doit ignored nuke
YeS NuKe IgNoreD
.SH DBPATH
By default
.B doit
writes its database to
.B .doit/doit.db
relative to the nearest ancestor directory that contains
.BR .doit/ .
The connection is opened with WAL journal mode and foreign keys
enabled.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.RS
doit init
doit add "buy milk"
doit add "call mom"
doit add taskA -t=2h , "task B" -t=1mo   # add two tasks at once
doit list
doit d 0            # mark the first listed todo done
doit c list         # show completed tasks
doit t list         # show trashed tasks
doit mv t 0 c       # move the first trashed task to completed
doit nuke           # permanently clear the todo list
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B doit
exits 0 on success. It exits 1 on usage errors, invalid confirmation input,
and out-of-range list indices, and 2 on a Go panic (see
.BR BUGS ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .doit/doit.db
The SQLite database created by
.BR "doit init" .
.SH BUGS
The following defects are present in this release:
.TP
.B trash remove deletes from the wrong list
.B doit t rm <id>
operates on the
.I completed
table instead of the
.I trash
table, so it reports success while the task stays in the trash.
.TP
.B Off-by-one index bounds checking
Index validation rejects indices greater than the list length but accepts an
index equal to it, which can panic with an out-of-range error or act on an
unrelated row.
.TP
.B Non-numeric ids are accepted by done
.B doit d <id>
warns about an unparsable id but does not abort, so it then operates on index 0
and exits 0.
.TP
.B Deadlines are parsed but not stored
The
.B -t=
value in
.B add
is validated and then discarded; no
.B expires_at
value is written and no expiration is enforced.
.TP
.B add with no task silently succeeds
Running
.B doit add
with no task argument prints nothing and exits 0.
.TP
.B Malformed multi-task input panics
A leading or trailing comma, or a second task argument that is not prefixed
with
.BR -t= ,
aborts with a Go panic and a stack trace instead of a clean error message.
.TP
.B Multi-session support is not yet fully active
The runtime session is hardcoded to
.IR todo ,
and the session subcommand is an unimplemented placeholder.
